1. The essential kubectl commands
      get — View resources
      apply — Reconcile resources with YAML
      create — Create a resource
      describe — Inspect a resource
      diff — Preview a configuration change
               kubectl diff -f office-web-deployment.yaml
      delete — Remove a resource
      replace - to replace and existing file/resouce after update
                kubectl replace --force -f filename

2. Commands for troubleshooting and monitoring
     logs --	Read application output 
              kubectl logs POD_NAME
     exec	--  Run a command inside a container
              kubectl exec -it POD_NAME -- sh
     port-forward --  Forward a local port to a resource
              kubectl port-forward deployment/office-web 8080:80  
     events  --  Inspect recent cluster events 
              kubectl get events
     top	--  View CPU and memory usage, if metrics are available
              kubectl top pods
     rollout --	Inspect or manage Deployment rollouts
              kubectl rollout status deployment/office-web

3. Commands that change a Deployment
      kubectl --  scale	Changes the number of replicas directly
      kubectl --  set image	Changes a workload's container image directly
      kubectl --  rollout status	Checks rollout progress
      kubectl --  rollout history	Lists previous Deployment revisions
      kubectl --  rollout undo	Rolls back to a previous revision
      kubectl --  rollout restart	Triggers a rolling restart

4. Commands for working with YAML
     kubectl apply -f file.yaml	  --  Apply configuration from a file
     kubectl diff -f file.yaml	  --  Preview differences
     kubectl create -f file.yaml	 -  Create resources from a file; fails if they already exist
     kubectl delete -f file.yaml	  -- Delete resources identified by the file
     kubectl get deployment office-web -o yaml	  --  Display a resource in YAML
     kubectl apply --dry-run=client -f file.yaml	--  Client-side dry-run validation
     kubectl explain deployment.spec	 --   Learn what a resource field means 

